export const OVERVIEW = "SITE: \"Abandoned but not Forgotten\" by Team Avantgarde for NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026 (https://codemicronx.github.io/nasa-Avantgarde/). Theme: hardware humans left behind on the Moon, Mars, in Earth orbit and deep space, why missions end and how to prevent space junk. SECTIONS (open them from the \"Explore\" button top-right): #moon Moon Mission Control — a real LRO-based 3D Moon; drag to spin, tap glowing pins, switch Surface/Terrain (LOLA height colours), and move the YEAR SLIDER from 1966 to today to watch each mission land, work, go silent, get lost or be found again; a mission file panel shows details. #game Survival Simulator — 3 playable campaigns (InSight, Spirit, Surveyor 3): make the real engineers' decisions to keep a spacecraft alive. #story Scroll story — 10 Moon/Mars chapters with a globe that pins each site. #catalog Museum — 47 story cards; filter by Retired / Lost & failed, by place and by cause; click a card for the full story, 3D model and sources. #atlas Ghost-site map on real NASA tiles (Moon LRO WAC, Mars Viking/MOLA, Earth GIBS); lavender pins = retired hardware, pink = lost. #lab 3D Lab — rotate 18 models. #orbit Live Orbit — real debris clouds (Kosmos 2251, Iridium 33, Fengyun-1C) placed with the SGP4 model from CelesTrak data; hover a dot to identify it, load live elements. #why Why missions end (9 causes). #numbers Debris numbers (NASA ODPO). #prevent Prevention checklist. #disclosure How it was made (AI disclosure). #sources Every fact, linked. Pip (me) is a draggable robot guide; tap ASK to chat. MUSEUM CARD IDS (id: name, year, place): vanguard-1: Vanguard 1, 1958, orbit, lost; ed-white-glove: Ed White’s spare glove, 1965, orbit, lost; surveyor-1: Surveyor 1, 1966, moon; surveyor-3: Surveyor 3, 1967, moon; surveyor-7: Surveyor 7, 1968, moon; luna-15: Luna 15, 1969, moon, lost; apollo-11-site: Tranquility Base leftovers, 1969, moon; apollo-12-site: Apollo 12: Intrepid & its ALSEP, 1969, moon; apollo-15-hammer: The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather, 1971, moon; apollo-14-golf: Alan Shepard’s golf balls, 1971, moon; mars-2: Mars 2 lander, 1971, mars, lost; mars-3: Mars 3 lander, 1971, mars, lost; apollo-15-lrv: Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle, 1971, moon; apollo-16-site: Apollo 16 site (Descartes), 1972, moon; apollo-17-site: Apollo 17 site (Taurus–Littrow), 1972, moon; viking-2: Viking 2 lander, 1976, mars; skylab: Skylab, 1979, earth, lost; viking-1: Viking 1 lander, 1982, mars; mars-observer: Mars Observer, 1993, orbit, lost; clementine: Clementine, 1994, elsewhere, lost; pioneer-11: Pioneer 11, 1995, elsewhere; mars-pathfinder: Mars Pathfinder & Sojourner, 1997, mars; mco: Mars Climate Orbiter, 1999, orbit, lost; mpl: Mars Polar Lander, 1999, mars, lost; mir: Mir space station, 2001, earth; near-shoemaker: NEAR Shoemaker, 2001, elsewhere; beagle-2: Beagle 2, 2003, mars, lost; pioneer-10: Pioneer 10, 2003, elsewhere; mgs: Mars Global Surveyor, 2006, orbit, lost; fengyun-1c: Fengyun-1C debris cloud, 2007, orbit, lost; piper-bag: The lost tool bag, 2008, orbit, lost; phoenix: Phoenix lander, 2008, mars; iridium-kosmos: Iridium 33 × Kosmos 2251, 2009, orbit, lost; apollo-11-tapes: The original Apollo 11 tapes, 2009, archive, lost; spirit: Spirit rover, 2010, mars; envisat: Envisat, 2012, orbit, lost; philae: Philae, 2014, elsewhere, lost; schiaparelli: Schiaparelli, 2016, mars, lost; opportunity: Opportunity rover, 2018, mars; insight: InSight lander, 2018, mars; dawn: Dawn, 2018, elsewhere; kepler: Kepler Space Telescope, 2018, elsewhere; beresheet: Beresheet, 2019, moon, lost; vikram: Vikram (Chandrayaan-2), 2019, moon, lost; hakuto-r: HAKUTO-R Mission 1, 2023, moon, lost; luna-25: Luna 25, 2023, moon, lost; ingenuity: Ingenuity Mars Helicopter, 2024, mars. COUNTS: Earth orbit: 9 cards, Earth: 2 cards, Moon: 15 cards, Mars: 13 cards, Deep space: 7 cards, Archives on Earth: 1 cards. The museum is a curated selection, not a complete list of everything humans left there. MOON MISSION CONTROL PINS: Luna 9 1966, Surveyor 1 1966, Surveyor 3 1967, Apollo 11 1969, Apollo 12 1969, Lunokhod 1 1970, Apollo 15 1971, Apollo 17 1972, Chandrayaan-3 2023.";
export const DOCS: { id: string; type: string; title: string; keys: string; text: string; place?: string; url?: string; link?: { label: string; target: string } | null }[] = [{"id":"vanguard-1","type":"card","title":"Vanguard 1","place":"orbit","keys":"Vanguard 1 vanguard 1 авангард вэнгард орбита земли","text":"Vanguard 1 (USA (Naval Research Laboratory); Earth orbit; 17 March 1958 (launch)). Lost or failed — status: Silent, still in orbit. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The oldest human-made object still orbiting Earth. A grapefruit-sized sphere with six antennas, Vanguard 1 was the fourth artificial satellite and the first to use solar cells. Its radio fell silent in 1964, but it is still up there: the oldest piece of human hardware in orbit. Below, its live position is computed from today’s CelesTrak orbital elements. Launched: 17 March 1958; First: solar-powered satellite; Status: defunct, still in orbit. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA — Vanguard Satellite, 1958 <https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/vanguard-satellite-1958/> | U.S. Naval Research Laboratory — 65-year milestone (communication ceased 1964) <https://www.nrl.navy.mil/Media/News/Article/3332003/nrl-achieves-65-year-milestone-in-space-satellite-exploration> | CelesTrak — current orbital elements (NORAD 5) <https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/gp.php?CATNR=5&FORMAT=tle>","link":{"label":"Vanguard 1","target":"#catalog?item=vanguard-1"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/vanguard-satellite-1958/"},{"id":"ed-white-glove","type":"card","title":"Ed White’s spare glove","place":"orbit","keys":"Ed White’s spare glove ed white glove эд уайт перчатка джемини орбита земли","text":"Ed White’s spare glove (NASA; Earth orbit; June 1965 (Gemini 4)). Lost or failed — status: Drifted away into orbit. Cause category: Human error during spacewalks. During the first American spacewalk, a spare glove floated out of the open hatch. On Gemini 4 Ed White became the first American to walk in space. While the hatch was open, a spare thermal glove drifted out of the open hatch and away into orbit — one of the very first objects lost by human hands in space. Mission: Gemini 4; Crew: James McDivitt, Ed White; Object: spare EVA glove. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum — Our favorite facts about the first U.S. spacewalk <https://airandspace.si.edu/stories/editorial/walking-space-our-favorite-facts-about-first-us-spacewalk>","link":{"label":"Ed White’s spare glove","target":"#catalog?item=ed-white-glove"},"url":"https://airandspace.si.edu/stories/editorial/walking-space-our-favorite-facts-about-first-us-spacewalk"},{"id":"luna-15","type":"card","title":"Luna 15","place":"moon","keys":"Luna 15 luna 15 луна-15 луна 15 луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Luna 15 (USSR; Moon; 21 July 1969). Lost or failed — status: Crashed. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. A Soviet robot that crashed into Mare Crisium while Apollo 11 astronauts were on the Moon. Launched days before Apollo 11, Luna 15 was trying to land and bring lunar soil back to Earth. On 21 July 1969 — while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface — it struck the Moon in Mare Crisium. Two rival programs, one Moon, the same day. Impact: 21 July 1969; Location: ≈17° N, 60° E (Mare Crisium). Coordinates: 17, 60. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Luna 15","target":"#catalog?item=luna-15"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html"},{"id":"apollo-11-site","type":"card","title":"Tranquility Base leftovers","place":"moon","keys":"Tranquility Base leftovers apollo 11 site аполлон-11 аполлон 11 армстронг олдрин база спокойствия море спокойствия флаг луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Tranquility Base leftovers (NASA; Moon; 20 July 1969). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Left on the surface on purpose. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The Apollo 11 descent stage and everything left beside it. To save weight for the trip home, the first Moon walkers left equipment behind: the lunar module descent stage, experiments, tools and the flag. Six Apollo descent stages still stand on the Moon — not lost, but abandoned, and now some of the most carefully mapped artefacts in the Solar System. Landed: 20 July 1969; Location: 0.674° N, 23.473° E. Coordinates: 0.67416, 23.47314. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html> | NASA NSSDCA — Apollo 11 Lunar Module <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-059C>","link":{"label":"Tranquility Base leftovers","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-11-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html"},{"id":"apollo-15-hammer","type":"card","title":"The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather","place":"moon","keys":"The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather apollo 15 hammer молоток перо галилей аполлон-15 скотт луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather (NASA; Moon; 2 August 1971). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Still on the surface. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. Dave Scott dropped a hammer and a feather to prove Galileo right — and left both behind. At Hadley–Apennine, astronaut David Scott held a geologic hammer and a falcon feather and let them go together. With no air, both hit the ground at the same moment. The experiment lasted a second; the hammer and feather are still lying where they fell. Site: Hadley–Apennine; Location: 26.132° N, 3.633° E. Coordinates: 26.13239, 3.6333. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA NSSDCA — The Apollo 15 Hammer-Feather Drop <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/apollo_15_feather_drop.html> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-15-hammer"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/apollo_15_feather_drop.html"},{"id":"apollo-14-golf","type":"card","title":"Alan Shepard’s golf balls","place":"moon","keys":"Alan Shepard’s golf balls apollo 14 golf гольф мячи шепард аполлон-14 луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Alan Shepard’s golf balls (NASA; Moon; February 1971). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Still on the surface. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. Apollo 14 commander Alan Shepard hit two golf balls at Fra Mauro and left them there. Using a makeshift club head attached to a sampling tool, Alan Shepard hit golf balls on the Moon. They are among the strangest objects humanity has left on another world. Site: Fra Mauro; Location: 3.646° S, 17.472° W. Coordinates: -3.64589, -17.47194. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA — Apollo 14 mission page <https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-14/> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Alan Shepard’s golf balls","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-14-golf"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-14/"},{"id":"mars-2","type":"card","title":"Mars 2 lander","place":"mars","keys":"Mars 2 lander mars 2 марс-2 марс 2 марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Mars 2 lander (USSR; Mars; 27 November 1971). Lost or failed — status: Crashed — never found. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. The first human-made object to reach the surface of Mars — it crashed. The Mars 2 lander entered the Martian atmosphere too steeply and crashed. Its impact made it the first human-made object to touch Mars. Its exact resting place has never been found. Crashed: 27 November 1971; Location: near 45° S, 58° E (Hellas Chaos). Coordinates: -45, 58. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf>","link":{"label":"Mars 2 lander","target":"#catalog?item=mars-2"},"url":"https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf"},{"id":"mars-3","type":"card","title":"Mars 3 lander","place":"mars","keys":"Mars 3 lander mars 3 марс-3 марс 3 марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Mars 3 lander (USSR; Mars; 2 December 1971). Lost or failed — status: Landed, then silent. Cause category: Loss of communication. The first soft landing on Mars — followed by silence within seconds. Mars 3 achieved the first soft landing on Mars and began transmitting, but the signal stopped after about 20 seconds. A planet-wide dust storm was raging. Nobody knows exactly why it went quiet. Landed: 2 December 1971; Transmission: ≈20 seconds; Location: 45.04° S, 202.02° E (Terra Sirenum). Coordinates: -45.044, -157.981. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf> | NASA Solar System Exploration — Mars 3 In Depth (archived) <https://web.archive.org/web/20190417185116/https:/solarsystem.nasa.gov/missions/mars-03/in-depth/>","link":{"label":"Mars 3 lander","target":"#catalog?item=mars-3"},"url":"https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf"},{"id":"skylab","type":"card","title":"Skylab","place":"earth","keys":"Skylab skylab скайлэб скайлab земля","text":"Skylab (NASA; Earth; 11 July 1979 (re-entry)). Lost or failed — status: Re-entered uncontrolled. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. America’s first space station fell back to Earth, scattering debris over Western Australia. Skylab hosted three crews in 1973–74 and was then left empty. On 11 July 1979 it re-entered and broke apart over the Indian Ocean and sparsely populated Western Australia; debris fell from around the town of Esperance (marked on the map) towards the Nullarbor Plain. Launched: 14 May 1973; Re-entry: 11 July 1979; Debris: Western Australia. Coordinates: -33.86, 121.89. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA — 45 Years Ago: Skylab Reenters Earth’s Atmosphere <https://www.nasa.gov/history/45-years-ago-skylab-reenters-earths-atmosphere/>","link":{"label":"Skylab","target":"#catalog?item=skylab"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/45-years-ago-skylab-reenters-earths-atmosphere/"},{"id":"viking-1","type":"card","title":"Viking 1 lander","place":"mars","keys":"Viking 1 lander viking 1 викинг-1 викинг 1 марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Viking 1 lander (NASA; Mars; 11 November 1982 (last contact)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent since 1982. Cause category: Software & command errors. After six years of work on Mars, a faulty command from Earth cut the link. Viking 1 landed in Chryse Planitia in 1976 and worked for more than six years. NASA’s mission page records that it operated until 11 November 1982, when it stopped working after receiving a faulty command from Earth. (The NSSDCA catalogue gives 13 November 1982 as the date contact was lost.) Landed: 20 July 1976; Last contact: 11 November 1982; Location: 22.27° N, 312.05° E. Coordinates: 22.269, -47.952. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model. Sources: NASA Science — Viking 1 mission page <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-1/> | NASA NSSDCA — Viking 1 Lander <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1975-075C> | The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf>","link":{"label":"Viking 1 lander","target":"#catalog?item=viking-1"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-1/"},{"id":"mars-observer","type":"card","title":"Mars Observer","place":"orbit","keys":"Mars Observer mars observer марс обсервер орбита земли","text":"Mars Observer (NASA; Earth orbit; 21 August 1993). Lost or failed — status: Lost before orbit insertion. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. Contact was lost three days before it was due to enter Mars orbit. Mars Observer went silent on 21 August 1993 while pressurising its propellant tanks before orbit insertion. The investigation concluded the most probable cause was a rupture in the propulsion system’s fuel pressurisation line. Lost: 21 August 1993; Probable cause: propellant pressurisation failure. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA JPL — Potential causes of the loss of Mars Observer identified <https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/potential-causes-of-the-loss-of-mars-observer-identified/> | NASA NSSDCA — Mars Observer summary <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/text/marsob.txt>","link":{"label":"Mars Observer","target":"#catalog?item=mars-observer"},"url":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/potential-causes-of-the-loss-of-mars-observer-identified/"},{"id":"clementine","type":"card","title":"Clementine","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Clementine clementine клементина дальний космос","text":"Clementine (BMDO / NASA; Deep space; 7 May 1994). Lost or failed — status: Asteroid flyby cancelled. Cause category: Software & command errors. A software malfunction fired a thruster until the fuel was gone. Clementine mapped the Moon in 1994 and then set off for asteroid Geographos. A malfunction caused one of its thrusters to fire until it used up its fuel, leaving the spacecraft spinning. The asteroid flyby never happened. Malfunction: 7 May 1994; Planned target: asteroid 1620 Geographos. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Clementine Project Information <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/clementine.html> | NASA APPEL — A software error took Clementine for a spin <https://appel.nasa.gov/2017/05/12/this-month-in-nasa-history-a-software-error-took-clementine-for-a-spin/>","link":{"label":"Clementine","target":"#catalog?item=clementine"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/clementine.html"},{"id":"mars-pathfinder","type":"card","title":"Mars Pathfinder & Sojourner","place":"mars","keys":"Mars Pathfinder & Sojourner mars pathfinder патфайндер соджорнер марс пасфайндер марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Mars Pathfinder & Sojourner (NASA; Mars; 27 September 1997 (last contact)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent since 1997. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. The lander that carried the first Mars rover fell silent after three months. Pathfinder bounced to a landing on airbags in Ares Vallis on 4 July 1997 and released Sojourner, the first rover on another planet. The last transmission came on 27 September 1997, and the lander was never heard from again. Landed: 4 July 1997; Last contact: 27 September 1997; Location: 19.33° N, 326.47° E. Coordinates: 19.33, -33.53. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf> | NASA Science — Mars Pathfinder <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-pathfinder/>","link":{"label":"Mars Pathfinder & Sojourner","target":"#catalog?item=mars-pathfinder"},"url":"https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf"},{"id":"mco","type":"card","title":"Mars Climate Orbiter","place":"orbit","keys":"Mars Climate Orbiter mco марс климат орбитер mars climate orbiter единицы фунты орбита земли","text":"Mars Climate Orbiter (NASA / JPL; Earth orbit; 23 September 1999). Lost or failed — status: Destroyed at Mars. Cause category: Unit & specification errors. Lost because one team used imperial units and another used metric. Ground software produced thruster data in pound-force seconds, while the navigation software expected newton-seconds. For months the error grew quietly. On arrival on 23 September 1999 the orbiter flew far too low into the Martian atmosphere and was never heard from again. Lost: 23 September 1999; Root cause: imperial vs. metric units; Lesson: verify every interface. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA — Mars Climate Orbiter Mishap Investigation Board Phase I Report (1999) <https://llis.nasa.gov/llis_lib/pdf/1009464main1_0641-mr.pdf> | NASA Science — Mars Climate Orbiter <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-climate-orbiter/>","link":{"label":"Mars Climate Orbiter","target":"#catalog?item=mco"},"url":"https://llis.nasa.gov/llis_lib/pdf/1009464main1_0641-mr.pdf"},{"id":"mpl","type":"card","title":"Mars Polar Lander","place":"mars","keys":"Mars Polar Lander mpl марс полар лендер полярный марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Mars Polar Lander (NASA / JPL; Mars; 3 December 1999). Lost or failed — status: Lost — never found. Cause category: Software & command errors. A vibration from the landing legs was probably mistaken for touchdown. Mars Polar Lander was never heard from after entry. The review board found the most probable cause: when the legs deployed, sensors produced a spurious touchdown signal; the software believed it and shut the engines off while the lander was still high above the ground. Lost: 3 December 1999; Target: near 76.6° S, 165.2° E; Probable cause: spurious touchdown signal. Coordinates: -76.57, 165.2. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf> | NASA Science — Mars Polar Lander / Deep Space 2 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-polar-lander-deep-space-2/>","link":{"label":"Mars Polar Lander","target":"#catalog?item=mpl"},"url":"https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf"},{"id":"mir","type":"card","title":"Mir space station","place":"earth","keys":"Mir space station mir мир станция земля","text":"Mir space station (Russia; Earth; 23 March 2001). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Deorbited on purpose. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. A controlled ending: Mir was steered into the remote South Pacific. Not every ending is a loss. After 15 years in orbit, Mir was deliberately guided to re-enter over the South Pacific — the region around Point Nemo (marker), the place on Earth farthest from land, used as a “spacecraft cemetery”. It is an example of how to end a mission responsibly. Launched: 1986 (core module); Deorbited: 23 March 2001; Area: South Pacific Ocean. Coordinates: -48.88, -123.39. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA — 20 Years Ago: Space Station Mir Reenters Earth’s Atmosphere <https://www.nasa.gov/history/20-years-ago-space-station-mir-reenters-earths-atmosphere/> | NOAA — Where is Point Nemo? <https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/point-nemo.html>","link":{"label":"Mir space station","target":"#catalog?item=mir"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/20-years-ago-space-station-mir-reenters-earths-atmosphere/"},{"id":"beagle-2","type":"card","title":"Beagle 2","place":"mars","keys":"Beagle 2 beagle 2 бигль-2 бигль марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Beagle 2 (UK / ESA (Mars Express); Mars; 25 December 2003). Lost or failed — status: Found in 2015. Cause category: Loss of communication. Lost on Christmas Day 2003 — found eleven years later, almost intact. Beagle 2 was released by ESA’s Mars Express and never called home. In 2015 NASA’s HiRISE camera spotted it in Isidis Planitia: it had landed, but not all of its solar panels opened, and the closed panels blocked its antenna. Lost, and then found. Expected landing: 25 December 2003; Found: January 2015 (HiRISE); Location: 11.529° N, 90.431° E. Coordinates: 11.52879, 90.43139. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: Bridges et al. (2017) — Identification of the Beagle 2 lander on Mars, Royal Society Open Science <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.170785> | NASA Science — Beagle 2 Lander on Mars, With Panels Deployed <https://science.nasa.gov/resource/beagle-2-lander-on-mars-with-panels-deployed/>","link":{"label":"Beagle 2","target":"#catalog?item=beagle-2"},"url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.170785"},{"id":"mgs","type":"card","title":"Mars Global Surveyor","place":"orbit","keys":"Mars Global Surveyor mgs марс глобал сервейер орбита земли","text":"Mars Global Surveyor (NASA / JPL; Earth orbit; November 2006). Lost or failed — status: Lost in Mars orbit. Cause category: Software & command errors. A command written to the wrong memory address led to a dead battery. After nearly ten years mapping Mars, Mars Global Surveyor went silent in November 2006. The review board traced it to a software update months earlier that wrote data to the wrong memory address; this set off a chain of faults: the spacecraft turned so that one battery faced direct sunlight, it overheated, and both batteries were drained within hours of the last contact on 2 November 2006. Arrived at Mars: September 1997; Lost: November 2006; Root cause: faulty memory upload. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA JPL — Report reveals likely causes of Mars spacecraft loss (2007) <https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/report-reveals-likely-causes-of-mars-spacecraft-loss/> | NASA Science — Mars Global Surveyor <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-global-surveyor/>","link":{"label":"Mars Global Surveyor","target":"#catalog?item=mgs"},"url":"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/report-reveals-likely-causes-of-mars-spacecraft-loss/"},{"id":"fengyun-1c","type":"card","title":"Fengyun-1C debris cloud","place":"orbit","keys":"Fengyun-1C debris cloud fengyun 1c фэнъюнь фенъюн орбита земли","text":"Fengyun-1C debris cloud (China; Earth orbit; 11 January 2007). Lost or failed — status: Thousands of fragments in orbit. Cause category: Collisions & fragmentation. A deliberate anti-satellite test that created one of the largest debris clouds ever. In 2007 the retired weather satellite Fengyun-1C was destroyed in an anti-satellite test. NASA’s Orbital Debris Program Office lists it among the events that most increased large debris in Earth orbit. The live counter below shows how many of its tracked fragments CelesTrak still lists today. Event: 11 January 2007; Type: intentional destruction. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA ODPO — FAQ (principal sources of large debris) <https://orbitaldebris.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/> | CelesTrak — Fengyun-1C debris elements <https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/gp.php?GROUP=fengyun-1c-debris&FORMAT=tle>","link":{"label":"Fengyun-1C debris cloud","target":"#catalog?item=fengyun-1c"},"url":"https://orbitaldebris.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/"},{"id":"piper-bag","type":"card","title":"The lost tool bag","place":"orbit","keys":"The lost tool bag piper bag сумка инструменты пайпер орбита земли","text":"The lost tool bag (NASA; Earth orbit; 18 November 2008 (STS-126)). Lost or failed — status: Drifted away. Cause category: Human error during spacewalks. A tool bag slipped away during a spacewalk outside the ISS — and was seen from the ground. While cleaning a grease gun leak during an STS-126 spacewalk, astronaut Heidemarie Stefanyshyn-Piper lost hold of a crew-lock tool bag. It drifted away; its contents included two grease guns, a scraper and wipes. NASA used backup tools to finish the work. Mission: STS-126; Object: crew lock bag with grease guns; Fate: drifted away into orbit. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA — STS-126 Space Shuttle Mission Report (via ibiblio) <https://www.ibiblio.org/apollo/Shuttle/Reports/Mission%20Reports/STS-126%20Space%20Shuttle%20Mission%20Report.pdf>","link":{"label":"The lost tool bag","target":"#catalog?item=piper-bag"},"url":"https://www.ibiblio.org/apollo/Shuttle/Reports/Mission%20Reports/STS-126%20Space%20Shuttle%20Mission%20Report.pdf"},{"id":"iridium-kosmos","type":"card","title":"Iridium 33 × Kosmos 2251","place":"orbit","keys":"Iridium 33 × Kosmos 2251 iridium kosmos иридиум космос-2251 столкновение орбита земли","text":"Iridium 33 × Kosmos 2251 (USA (commercial) / Russia; Earth orbit; 10 February 2009). Lost or failed — status: Two debris clouds. Cause category: Collisions & fragmentation. The first accidental collision between two intact satellites. An active Iridium communications satellite and a dead Russian Kosmos satellite met at orbital speed. Both were shattered. NASA cites this collision as one of the major events that increased large debris in Earth orbit — the live globe shows the fragments still being tracked. Collision: 10 February 2009; Objects: Iridium 33 (active), Kosmos 2251 (defunct). 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA ODPO — FAQ <https://orbitaldebris.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/> | NASA ODPO — Orbital Debris Quarterly News 13-2 (April 2009) <https://orbitaldebris.jsc.nasa.gov/quarterly-news/pdfs/odqnv13i2.pdf>","link":{"label":"Iridium 33 × Kosmos 2251","target":"#catalog?item=iridium-kosmos"},"url":"https://orbitaldebris.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/"},{"id":"spirit","type":"card","title":"Spirit rover","place":"mars","keys":"Spirit rover spirit спирит марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Spirit rover (NASA / JPL; Mars; 22 March 2010 (last contact)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Stuck in sand, silent. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. Trapped in soft soil, Spirit could not tilt its panels toward the winter Sun. Spirit worked for six years in Gusev Crater, far beyond its 90-day plan. In 2009 it became stuck in soft sand. Unable to angle its solar panels for the Martian winter, it ran out of power; the last contact was on 22 March 2010. Landed: 4 January 2004; Last contact: 22 March 2010; Roved: 7.73 km. Coordinates: -14.5692, 175.4729. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf> | NASA Science — Spirit <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mer-spirit/>","link":{"label":"Spirit rover","target":"#catalog?item=spirit"},"url":"https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf"},{"id":"envisat","type":"card","title":"Envisat","place":"orbit","keys":"Envisat envisat энвисат орбита земли","text":"Envisat (ESA; Earth orbit; 8 April 2012). Lost or failed — status: Dead, still in orbit. Cause category: Loss of communication. A large Earth observation satellite that suddenly stopped talking after ten years in orbit. Envisat watched Earth for ten years. On 8 April 2012 ESA lost contact with it and could not recover it. Today it is still in orbit as a dead satellite — exactly the kind of object that active debris removal missions are being designed to deal with. Launched: 1 March 2002; Contact lost: 8 April 2012. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: ESA — ESA declares end of mission for Envisat <https://www.esa.int/Applications/Observing_the_Earth/Envisat/ESA_declares_end_of_mission_for_Envisat>","link":{"label":"Envisat","target":"#catalog?item=envisat"},"url":"https://www.esa.int/Applications/Observing_the_Earth/Envisat/ESA_declares_end_of_mission_for_Envisat"},{"id":"philae","type":"card","title":"Philae","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Philae philae филы комета розетта дальний космос","text":"Philae (ESA / DLR; Deep space; 12 November 2014). Lost or failed — status: Found in 2016. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. The first landing on a comet bounced into the shadows. Rosetta’s lander Philae touched down on comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko, but it bounced and flew for another two hours before coming to rest at a shadowed site later named Abydos. After three days its primary battery was exhausted. In September 2016, weeks before the end of the mission, Rosetta finally photographed it. Landing: 12 November 2014; Found: September 2016; Where: comet 67P. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: ESA — Philae found! <https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Rosetta/Philae_found>","link":{"label":"Philae","target":"#catalog?item=philae"},"url":"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Rosetta/Philae_found"},{"id":"schiaparelli","type":"card","title":"Schiaparelli","place":"mars","keys":"Schiaparelli schiaparelli скиапарелли марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Schiaparelli (ESA (ExoMars); Mars; 19 October 2016). Lost or failed — status: Crashed. Cause category: Software & command errors. A one-second sensor saturation made the computer think it was already on the ground. During descent, Schiaparelli’s inertial measurement unit saturated for about one second. The guidance software combined the bad value with other data, computed a negative altitude, released the parachute early and fired the thrusters for only a few seconds. The lander fell from kilometres up. Crash: 19 October 2016; Location: 2.07° S, 353.79° E; Root cause: IMU saturation + software logic. Coordinates: -2.07, -6.21. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: ESA — Schiaparelli landing investigation completed <https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Human_and_Robotic_Exploration/Exploration/ExoMars/Schiaparelli_landing_investigation_completed> | ESA — Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter views Schiaparelli landing site <https://www.esa.int/articles/about-us/meet-us/sites-and-establishments/esa-esac/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter-views-schiaparelli-landing-site>","link":{"label":"Schiaparelli","target":"#catalog?item=schiaparelli"},"url":"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Human_and_Robotic_Exploration/Exploration/ExoMars/Schiaparelli_landing_investigation_completed"},{"id":"opportunity","type":"card","title":"Opportunity rover","place":"mars","keys":"Opportunity rover opportunity оппортьюнити оппи марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Opportunity rover (NASA / JPL; Mars; 10 June 2018 (last contact)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Mission ended 2019. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. A planet-wide dust storm blocked the sunlight its solar panels needed. Planned for 90 days, Opportunity drove 45 km over almost 15 years. In June 2018 a global dust storm darkened the sky; the rover’s last message arrived on 10 June. After hundreds of recovery commands NASA declared the mission complete in February 2019. Its 45.16 km held the record for driving on another world until 11 October 2026, when NASA’s Perseverance rover passed it. (Marker: its landing site in Meridiani Planum.) Landed: 24 January 2004; Last contact: 10 June 2018; Roved: 45.16 km. Coordinates: -1.9462, -5.5266. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model. Sources: NASA — NASA’s record-setting Opportunity rover mission on Mars comes to end <https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-record-setting-opportunity-rover-mission-on-mars-comes-to-end/> | NASA Science — Mars Exploration Rovers: Spirit and Opportunity (distance record, 2026) <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-exploration-rovers-spirit-and-opportunity/> | The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf>","link":{"label":"Opportunity rover","target":"#catalog?item=opportunity"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasas-record-setting-opportunity-rover-mission-on-mars-comes-to-end/"},{"id":"beresheet","type":"card","title":"Beresheet","place":"moon","keys":"Beresheet beresheet берешит израиль луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Beresheet (SpaceIL / IAI (Israel); Moon; 11 April 2019). Lost or failed — status: Crashed — found by LRO. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. The first privately funded Moon landing attempt ended in Mare Serenitatis. Beresheet reached lunar orbit and began its descent, but its main engine shut down during the landing burn and it hit the surface. Eleven days later NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photographed a dark smudge: the crash site. Impact: 11 April 2019; Location: 32.5956° N, 19.3496° E; Found by: LRO Camera. Coordinates: 32.5956, 19.3496. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: LROC — Beresheet Crash Site Spotted! <https://www.lroc.im-ldi.com/images/1101>","link":{"label":"Beresheet","target":"#catalog?item=beresheet"},"url":"https://www.lroc.im-ldi.com/images/1101"},{"id":"vikram","type":"card","title":"Vikram (Chandrayaan-2)","place":"moon","keys":"Vikram (Chandrayaan-2) vikram викрам чандраян-2 чандраян 2 луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Vikram (Chandrayaan-2) (ISRO (India); Moon; 6 September 2019). Lost or failed — status: Crashed — found by LRO. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. India’s first Moon lander lost contact moments before touchdown near the south pole. Vikram lost contact during its final descent. Weeks later, an amateur, Shanmuga Subramanian, studied NASA LRO images and pointed out debris; the LRO team confirmed the impact site and credited him. A lost object found by a citizen with open NASA data. Impact: 6 September 2019; Location: 70.881° S, 22.785° E; Found by: citizen + LRO. Coordinates: -70.881, 22.7849. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA — Vikram Lander Found <https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/vikram-lander-found/> | NASA GSFC PGDA — lunar retroreflector locations <https://pgda.gsfc.nasa.gov/products/101>","link":{"label":"Vikram (Chandrayaan-2)","target":"#catalog?item=vikram"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/vikram-lander-found/"},{"id":"hakuto-r","type":"card","title":"HAKUTO-R Mission 1","place":"moon","keys":"HAKUTO-R Mission 1 hakuto r хакуто ispace луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"HAKUTO-R Mission 1 (ispace (Japan); Moon; 25 April 2023). Lost or failed — status: Crashed — found by LRO. Cause category: Software & command errors. The lander misjudged its altitude and ran out of fuel above the surface. Planning to land near Atlas crater, HAKUTO-R’s software rejected correct altimeter readings as faulty after it passed over a crater rim. It believed it was on the surface while it was still about 5 km up, kept descending slowly until its propellant ran out, and fell. LRO found the debris a day later. Impact: 25 April 2023; Location: 47.581° N, 44.094° E. Coordinates: 47.581, 44.094. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA — LRO views impact site of HAKUTO-R Mission 1 <https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/goddard/nasas-lro-views-impact-site-of-hakuto-r-mission-1-moon-lander/> | ispace — Results of the HAKUTO-R Mission 1 lunar landing (26 May 2023) <https://www.ispace-inc.com/2023/05/26/ispace-announces-results-of-the-hakuto-r-mission-1-lunar-landing/>","link":{"label":"HAKUTO-R Mission 1","target":"#catalog?item=hakuto-r"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/goddard/nasas-lro-views-impact-site-of-hakuto-r-mission-1-moon-lander/"},{"id":"luna-25","type":"card","title":"Luna 25","place":"moon","keys":"Luna 25 luna 25 луна-25 луна 25 луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Luna 25 (Roscosmos (Russia); Moon; 19 August 2023). Lost or failed — status: Crashed — found by LRO. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. Russia’s first lunar mission in 47 years crashed before landing. During a manoeuvre before its planned descent, Luna 25’s engine did not perform as planned and the spacecraft struck the Moon. LRO found a fresh 10-metre crater on the rim of Pontécoulant G crater, about 400 km short of the intended landing site. Impact: 19 August 2023; Location: 57.865° S, 61.360° E; Crater: ≈10 m wide. Coordinates: -57.865, 61.36. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA — LRO observes crater likely from Luna 25 impact <https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/nasas-lro-observes-crater-likely-from-luna-25-impact/>","link":{"label":"Luna 25","target":"#catalog?item=luna-25"},"url":"https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/nasas-lro-observes-crater-likely-from-luna-25-impact/"},{"id":"apollo-11-tapes","type":"card","title":"The original Apollo 11 tapes","place":"archive","keys":"The original Apollo 11 tapes apollo 11 tapes плёнки записи кассеты аполлон-11 архив","text":"The original Apollo 11 tapes (NASA; Archives on Earth; search concluded 2009). Lost or failed — status: Most likely erased and reused. Cause category: Data & archive loss. The original high-quality recordings of the first Moon walk were probably recorded over. The Moon walk was broadcast from a slow-scan TV signal that was converted for television. The original telemetry tapes holding the higher-quality signal were searched for over three years. NASA concluded in 2009 that they were most likely erased and reused, and restored the best surviving broadcast copies instead. Search: 2006–2009; Result: tapes most likely erased and reused; Recovery: restored broadcast copies. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA — The Apollo 11 Telemetry Data Recordings: A Final Report (2009) <https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20110011709>","link":{"label":"The original Apollo 11 tapes","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-11-tapes"},"url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20110011709"},{"id":"surveyor-1","type":"card","title":"Surveyor 1","place":"moon","keys":"Surveyor 1 surveyor 1 сервейер-1 сервейер 1 сёрвейер луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Surveyor 1 (NASA / JPL; Moon; 2 June 1966). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent since January 1967. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The first true soft landing on the Moon by NASA — proof that a spacecraft could set down gently. Before astronauts could land, engineers had to know whether the Moon’s surface would swallow a spacecraft. Surveyor 1 answered by landing softly in Oceanus Procellarum on 2 June 1966 and sending back pictures. Contact ended on 7 January 1967. It is still standing where it landed. Landed: 2 June 1966; Contact ended: 7 January 1967; Location: 2.47° S, 43.34° W. Coordinates: -2.4745, -43.3398. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA Science — Surveyor 1 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-1/> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Surveyor 1","target":"#catalog?item=surveyor-1"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-1/"},{"id":"surveyor-3","type":"card","title":"Surveyor 3","place":"moon","keys":"Surveyor 3 surveyor 3 сервейер-3 сервейер 3 сёрвейер луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Surveyor 3 (NASA / JPL; Moon; 20 April 1967). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Visited by Apollo 12 in 1969. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The robot that tested lunar soil for Apollo — and was later visited by astronauts. Surveyor 3 bounced twice before settling, then dug trenches with a scoop and showed the soil could hold an Apollo lander. It fell silent two days into the lunar night. In November 1969 Apollo 12 landed nearby and the astronauts walked over to remove its camera and scoop. Play its campaign in the Simulator. Landed: 20 April 1967; Last contact: 4 May 1967; Location: 3.016° S, 23.418° W. Coordinates: -3.0162, -23.418. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Surveyor 3 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-3/> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Surveyor 3","target":"#catalog?item=surveyor-3"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-3/"},{"id":"surveyor-7","type":"card","title":"Surveyor 7","place":"moon","keys":"Surveyor 7 surveyor 7 сервейер-7 сервейер 7 тихо луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Surveyor 7 (NASA / JPL; Moon; 10 January 1968). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Resting near Tycho crater. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The last Surveyor landed in the rugged highlands beside the bright crater Tycho. With the Apollo landing zones scouted, Surveyor 7 went somewhere scientifically exciting instead: the ejecta blanket of Tycho crater, about 29 km north of the rim. It is the southernmost of the Surveyors. Landed: 10 January 1968; Location: 40.98° S, 11.51° W; Near: Tycho crater. Coordinates: -40.9812, -11.5127. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Surveyor 7 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-7/> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Surveyor 7","target":"#catalog?item=surveyor-7"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-7/"},{"id":"apollo-12-site","type":"card","title":"Apollo 12: Intrepid & its ALSEP","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 12: Intrepid & its ALSEP apollo 12 site аполлон-12 аполлон 12 интрепид alsep луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Apollo 12: Intrepid & its ALSEP (NASA; Moon; 19 November 1969). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Experiments switched off in 1977. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. A descent stage and a science station that kept reporting for almost eight years. Apollo 12’s lunar module Intrepid landed close to Surveyor 3. The crew set up an Apollo Lunar Surface Experiments Package (ALSEP) — a small science station powered without sunlight. It was turned on on 19 November 1969 and shut down with the other ALSEP stations on 30 September 1977. Landed: 19 November 1969; ALSEP on: 19 Nov 1969 – 30 Sep 1977; Location: 3.013° S, 23.422° W. Coordinates: -3.0128, -23.4219. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model. Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Apollo 12 Lunar Module / ALSEP <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Apollo 12: Intrepid & its ALSEP","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-12-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C"},{"id":"apollo-15-lrv","type":"card","title":"Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle apollo 15 lrv луномобиль lrv ровер аполлон-15 машина луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle (NASA; Moon; July–August 1971). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Parked permanently at Hadley–Apennine. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The first of three electric “moon buggies”, parked for good beside the Apennine mountains. Apollo 15’s lunar module Falcon carried the first Lunar Roving Vehicle, letting astronauts drive to distant geological targets. The rover, the descent stage and an ALSEP science station (active from 31 July 1971 until 30 September 1977) were all left behind. Marker: the LM descent-stage position. Landing: 30 July 1971; ALSEP active: 31 Jul 1971 – 30 Sep 1977; Location: 26.132° N, 3.633° E. Coordinates: 26.13239, 3.6333. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Apollo 15 Lunar Module / ALSEP (incl. LRV) <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-063C> | NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-15-lrv"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-063C"},{"id":"apollo-16-site","type":"card","title":"Apollo 16 site (Descartes)","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 16 site (Descartes) apollo 16 site аполлон-16 декарт луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Apollo 16 site (Descartes) (NASA; Moon; 21 April 1972). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Descent stage, rover & ALSEP left behind. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The only Apollo landing in the central lunar highlands. Apollo 16’s descent stage stands in the Descartes highlands with the second Lunar Roving Vehicle and another ALSEP science station nearby. Landed: 21 April 1972; Location: 8.973° S, 15.501° E. Coordinates: -8.9734, 15.5011. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model. Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Apollo 16 site (Descartes)","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-16-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html"},{"id":"apollo-17-site","type":"card","title":"Apollo 17 site (Taurus–Littrow)","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 17 site (Taurus–Littrow) apollo 17 site аполлон-17 таурус литтров сернан луна луне луну луны лунный","text":"Apollo 17 site (Taurus–Littrow) (NASA; Moon; 11 December 1972). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Last crewed landing — hardware still there. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The last time humans walked on the Moon; the third rover is parked here. When Apollo 17’s crew left in December 1972, they left the descent stage, the third Lunar Roving Vehicle and a science station in the valley of Taurus–Littrow. No human has been back since. Landed: 11 December 1972; Location: 20.191° N, 30.772° E. Coordinates: 20.1911, 30.7723. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model. Sources: NASA NSSDCA — Table of Anthropogenic Impacts and Spacecraft on the Moon <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html>","link":{"label":"Apollo 17 site (Taurus–Littrow)","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-17-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunar_artifact_impacts.html"},{"id":"viking-2","type":"card","title":"Viking 2 lander","place":"mars","keys":"Viking 2 lander viking 2 викинг-2 викинг 2 марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Viking 2 lander (NASA; Mars; 3 September 1976). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Mission ended April 1980. Cause category: Abandonment at end of life. The second Viking lander, sent to Utopia Planitia to search for signs of life. Viking 2 touched down on the plains of Utopia Planitia, chosen because water was located nearby and there was a better chance of finding signs of life. The lander mission ended on 12 April 1980. It still stands where it landed. Landed: 3 September 1976; Mission end: 12 April 1980; Location: 47.64° N, 134.29° E. Coordinates: 47.643, 134.288. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA Science — Viking 2 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-2/> | The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf>","link":{"label":"Viking 2 lander","target":"#catalog?item=viking-2"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-2/"},{"id":"phoenix","type":"card","title":"Phoenix lander","place":"mars","keys":"Phoenix lander phoenix феникс марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Phoenix lander (NASA / JPL / University of Arizona; Mars; 25 May 2008). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Mission ended 2 November 2008. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. A lander that dug into the Martian arctic, where winter ended its mission. Phoenix landed in Vastitas Borealis — the first successful landing of a stationary lander on Mars since Viking 2, about 32 years earlier. It dug into the arctic soil with its robotic arm. As the polar winter approached, sunlight faded; the mission ended on 2 November 2008. Landed: 25 May 2008; End of mission: 2 November 2008; Location: 68.22° N, 234.25° E. Coordinates: 68.2188, -125.7492. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Phoenix <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/phoenix/> | The Planetary Society — Every Mars Landing Attempt, Ever (E. Lakdawalla, 2020, v1.3) <https://planetary.s3.amazonaws.com/assets/images/charts-diagrams/2020/20200603_map-mars-landing-sites-2020-detailed_ver1-3.pdf>","link":{"label":"Phoenix lander","target":"#catalog?item=phoenix"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/phoenix/"},{"id":"insight","type":"card","title":"InSight lander","place":"mars","keys":"InSight lander insight инсайт сейсмометр марсотрясения марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"InSight lander (NASA / JPL; Mars; 26 November 2018). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent since 15 December 2022. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. The marsquake listener whose solar panels were slowly buried by dust. InSight placed a seismometer on Mars and recorded more than 1,300 marsquakes, including the largest ever detected on another planet. Dust on its solar panels cut its power to a tenth of what it had at landing, and it fell silent on 15 December 2022. Play its campaign in the Simulator. Landed: 26 November 2018; Last contact: 15 December 2022; Location: 4.502° N, 135.623° E. Coordinates: 4.502384, 135.623447. 3D: official NASA 3D Resources model (also in the 3D Lab). Sources: NASA Science — InSight <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/insight/> | NASA — NASA Retires InSight Mars Lander Mission <https://www.nasa.gov/missions/insight/nasa-retires-insight-mars-lander-mission-after-years-of-science/>","link":{"label":"InSight lander","target":"#catalog?item=insight"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/insight/"},{"id":"ingenuity","type":"card","title":"Ingenuity Mars Helicopter","place":"mars","keys":"Ingenuity Mars Helicopter ingenuity инджиньюити вертолёт вертолет марс марсе марса марсианский","text":"Ingenuity Mars Helicopter (NASA / JPL; Mars; 18 January 2024 (last flight)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Grounded in Jezero Crater since January 2024. Cause category: Entry, descent & landing failures. The first aircraft on another planet: planned for 5 flights, it flew 72. Ingenuity rode to Mars under the Perseverance rover. On 19 April 2021 it made the first powered, controlled flight on another planet. It was meant to fly at most five times in 30 days; instead it flew 72 times over nearly three years and became an aerial scout for the rover team. Its last flight was on 18 January 2024, and its rotor blades were damaged. NASA ended the mission on 25 January 2024. It still stands in Jezero Crater. First flight: 19 April 2021; Last flight: 18 January 2024 (flight 72); Flying time: 128.8 minutes, 17.0 km; Where: Jezero Crater, Mars. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Ingenuity Mars Helicopter <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-mars-helicopter/>","link":{"label":"Ingenuity Mars Helicopter","target":"#catalog?item=ingenuity"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-mars-helicopter/"},{"id":"near-shoemaker","type":"card","title":"NEAR Shoemaker","place":"elsewhere","keys":"NEAR Shoemaker near shoemaker нир шумейкер эрос астероид дальний космос","text":"NEAR Shoemaker (NASA / JHU APL; Deep space; 12 February 2001 (landing)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Resting on asteroid 433 Eros since 2001. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. The first spacecraft to orbit an asteroid, and then the first to land on one. NEAR reached asteroid 433 Eros and on 14 February 2000 became the first human-made object to orbit an asteroid. It was never designed to land, but on 12 February 2001 the team set it down gently on the surface. It kept sending data for about two weeks. The last contact was on 28 February 2001; the spacecraft had succumbed to the extreme cold. It is still sitting on Eros. Launched: 17 February 1996; Orbit of Eros: from 14 February 2000; Landed: 12 February 2001; Last contact: 28 February 2001; Images returned: about 160,000. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — NEAR Shoemaker <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/>","link":{"label":"NEAR Shoemaker","target":"#catalog?item=near-shoemaker"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/"},{"id":"dawn","type":"card","title":"Dawn","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Dawn dawn доун рассвет веста церера дальний космос","text":"Dawn (NASA / JPL; Deep space; 1 November 2018 (mission end)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent, in a stable orbit around dwarf planet Ceres. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. An ion-powered explorer that orbited two worlds and now circles Ceres in silence. Dawn used ion engines to visit the two largest bodies in the main asteroid belt. It orbited Vesta from 2011 and Ceres from 2015, the first spacecraft to orbit two destinations beyond Earth. In autumn 2018 it ran out of hydrazine, the fuel that kept it pointed at Earth, and missed its calls on 31 October and 1 November. To protect Ceres, NASA left it in an orbit expected to last at least 20 years. Launched: 27 September 2007; Vesta: arrived 2011; Ceres: arrived 2015; End: 1 November 2018 (out of hydrazine). 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Dawn <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/> | NASA JPL — NASA’s Dawn Mission to Asteroid Belt Comes to End (1 Nov 2018) <https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-dawn-mission-to-asteroid-belt-comes-to-end/>","link":{"label":"Dawn","target":"#catalog?item=dawn"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/"},{"id":"kepler","type":"card","title":"Kepler Space Telescope","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Kepler Space Telescope kepler кеплер телескоп экзопланеты дальний космос","text":"Kepler Space Telescope (NASA; Deep space; 30 October 2018 (retired)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Retired, drifting in a safe orbit around the Sun, away from Earth. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. The planet hunter that showed the night sky holds more planets than stars. Kepler stared at about 150,000 stars, watching for the tiny dip in light when a planet passes in front of its star. In 2013 it lost a second reaction wheel, so engineers used the pressure of sunlight to steady it and gave it a second life, K2. When its fuel ran out, NASA retired it on 30 October 2018 in its safe orbit, away from Earth. It left more than 2,600 planet discoveries. Launched: 6 March 2009 (US time); Retired: 30 October 2018; Planets found: more than 2,600. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Kepler / K2 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/kepler/>","link":{"label":"Kepler Space Telescope","target":"#catalog?item=kepler"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/kepler/"},{"id":"pioneer-10","type":"card","title":"Pioneer 10","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Pioneer 10 pioneer 10 пионер-10 пионер 10 дальний космос","text":"Pioneer 10 (NASA Ames; Deep space; 23 January 2003 (last signal)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent, leaving the Solar System towards Aldebaran. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. The first spacecraft through the asteroid belt and past Jupiter, now a silent ghost ship. Built for a 21-month trip to Jupiter, Pioneer 10 lasted more than 30 years. It was the first spacecraft to cross the asteroid belt and fly past Jupiter (December 1973). Its nuclear power source slowly decayed, and its last faint signal arrived on 23 January 2003 from 12.2 billion km away. It carries a metal plaque that tells anyone who finds it where it came from. Launched: 2 March 1972; Jupiter flyby: 4 December 1973; Last signal: 23 January 2003; Heading: towards Aldebaran (about 2 million years). 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Pioneer 10 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/pioneer-10/>","link":{"label":"Pioneer 10","target":"#catalog?item=pioneer-10"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/pioneer-10/"},{"id":"pioneer-11","type":"card","title":"Pioneer 11","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Pioneer 11 pioneer 11 пионер-11 пионер 11 дальний космос","text":"Pioneer 11 (NASA Ames; Deep space; 30 September 1995 (last contact)). Retired on purpose / mission ended — status: Silent, leaving the Solar System towards Sagittarius. Cause category: Fuel & power failures. The first spacecraft to fly past Saturn. Pioneer 10’s twin flew three times closer to Jupiter, then used Jupiter’s gravity to swing to Saturn. On 1 September 1979 it became the first spacecraft to fly past Saturn and found its F ring. NASA Ames last made contact on 30 September 1995. In 1995 its project manager called it a future “ghost ship in our galaxy”. Launched: 6 April 1973 (UT); Saturn flyby: 1 September 1979; Last contact: 30 September 1995. 3D: simplified procedural reconstruction. Sources: NASA Science — Pioneer 11 <https://science.nasa.gov/mission/pioneer-11/>","link":{"label":"Pioneer 11","target":"#catalog?item=pioneer-11"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/pioneer-11/"},{"id":"place-moon","type":"place","title":"Moon overview","place":"moon","keys":"Moon луна луне луну луны лунный overview всё обзор","text":"Moon — The Moon has no air, wind or rain, so hardware left there can last for millions of years. Humans have left landers, rovers, descent stages, experiment packages, laser mirrors and flags there; Apollo 11, 14 and 15 laser retroreflectors (and Lunokhod 1, Chandrayaan-3) are still used to measure the Earth–Moon distance (the Moon drifts away about 3.8 cm per year). Estimates based on NASA records put the human-made material on the Moon at roughly 190 tonnes (about 187,000 kg), from more than 100 crashed or landed objects of many countries; NASA’s NSSDCA keeps the table of impact and landing sites. The museum has 15 cards here: Surveyor 1 (1966, retired): The first true soft landing on the Moon by NASA — proof that a spacecraft could set down gently. Surveyor 3 (1967, retired): The robot that tested lunar soil for Apollo — and was later visited by astronauts. Surveyor 7 (1968, retired): The last Surveyor landed in the rugged highlands beside the bright crater Tycho. Luna 15 (1969, lost): A Soviet robot that crashed into Mare Crisium while Apollo 11 astronauts were on the Moon. Tranquility Base leftovers (1969, retired): The Apollo 11 descent stage and everything left beside it. Apollo 12: Intrepid & its ALSEP (1969, retired): A descent stage and a science station that kept reporting for almost eight years. The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather (1971, retired): Dave Scott dropped a hammer and a feather to prove Galileo right — and left both behind. Alan Shepard’s golf balls (1971, retired): Apollo 14 commander Alan Shepard hit two golf balls at Fra Mauro and left them there. Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle (1971, retired): The first of three electric “moon buggies”, parked for good beside the Apennine mountains. Apollo 16 site (Descartes) (1972, retired): The only Apollo landing in the central lunar highlands. Apollo 17 site (Taurus–Littrow) (1972, retired): The last time humans walked on the Moon; the third rover is parked here. Beresheet (2019, lost): The first privately funded Moon landing attempt ended in Mare Serenitatis. Vikram (Chandrayaan-2) (2019, lost): India’s first Moon lander lost contact moments before touchdown near the south pole. HAKUTO-R Mission 1 (2023, lost): The lander misjudged its altitude and ran out of fuel above the surface. Luna 25 (2023, lost): Russia’s first lunar mission in 47 years crashed before landing. Moon Mission Control also shows: Luna 9 (1966, Oceanus Procellarum (Ocean of Storms)); Surveyor 1 (1966, Inside a crater north of Flamsteed, Oceanus Procellarum); Surveyor 3 (1967, Oceanus Procellarum, on a 14° slope inside a small crater); Apollo 11 (1969, Tranquility Base, Mare Tranquillitatis (Sea of Tranquility)); Apollo 12 (1969, Oceanus Procellarum, ~180 m from Surveyor 3); Lunokhod 1 (1970, Mare Imbrium (Sea of Rains)); Apollo 15 (1971, Hadley–Apennine, at the foot of the Apennine mountains); Apollo 17 (1972, Taurus–Littrow valley); Chandrayaan-3 (2023, Near the lunar south pole, near Manzinus crater).","link":{"label":"Moon Mission Control","target":"#moon"}},{"id":"place-mars","type":"place","title":"Mars overview","place":"mars","keys":"Mars марс марсе марса марсианский overview всё обзор","text":"Mars — Mars has thin air, dust storms and cold nights; most solar-powered landers there ended when dust covered their panels or winter came, and several landers crashed or went silent during landing. The museum has 13 cards here: Mars 2 lander (1971, lost): The first human-made object to reach the surface of Mars — it crashed. Mars 3 lander (1971, lost): The first soft landing on Mars — followed by silence within seconds. Viking 2 lander (1976, retired): The second Viking lander, sent to Utopia Planitia to search for signs of life. Viking 1 lander (1982, retired): After six years of work on Mars, a faulty command from Earth cut the link. Mars Pathfinder & Sojourner (1997, retired): The lander that carried the first Mars rover fell silent after three months. Mars Polar Lander (1999, lost): A vibration from the landing legs was probably mistaken for touchdown. Beagle 2 (2003, lost): Lost on Christmas Day 2003 — found eleven years later, almost intact. Phoenix lander (2008, retired): A lander that dug into the Martian arctic, where winter ended its mission. Spirit rover (2010, retired): Trapped in soft soil, Spirit could not tilt its panels toward the winter Sun. Schiaparelli (2016, lost): A one-second sensor saturation made the computer think it was already on the ground. Opportunity rover (2018, retired): A planet-wide dust storm blocked the sunlight its solar panels needed. InSight lander (2018, retired): The marsquake listener whose solar panels were slowly buried by dust. Ingenuity Mars Helicopter (2024, retired): The first aircraft on another planet: planned for 5 flights, it flew 72.","link":{"label":"Mars on the Atlas map","target":"#atlas"}},{"id":"place-orbit","type":"place","title":"Earth orbit overview","place":"orbit","keys":"Earth orbit орбита земли overview всё обзор","text":"Earth orbit — In Earth orbit dead satellites and fragments keep moving at 7–8 km/s; collisions create clouds of debris (see Live Orbit and Numbers). The museum has 9 cards here: Vanguard 1 (1958, lost): The oldest human-made object still orbiting Earth. Ed White’s spare glove (1965, lost): During the first American spacewalk, a spare glove floated out of the open hatch. Mars Observer (1993, lost): Contact was lost three days before it was due to enter Mars orbit. Mars Climate Orbiter (1999, lost): Lost because one team used imperial units and another used metric. Mars Global Surveyor (2006, lost): A command written to the wrong memory address led to a dead battery. Fengyun-1C debris cloud (2007, lost): A deliberate anti-satellite test that created one of the largest debris clouds ever. The lost tool bag (2008, lost): A tool bag slipped away during a spacewalk outside the ISS — and was seen from the ground. Iridium 33 × Kosmos 2251 (2009, lost): The first accidental collision between two intact satellites. Envisat (2012, lost): A large Earth observation satellite that suddenly stopped talking after ten years in orbit.","link":{"label":"Museum catalog","target":"#catalog"}},{"id":"place-elsewhere","type":"place","title":"Deep space overview","place":"elsewhere","keys":"Deep space дальний космос overview всё обзор","text":"Deep space — In deep space, retired probes sit on asteroids and comets or fly out of the Solar System. The museum has 7 cards here: Clementine (1994, lost): A software malfunction fired a thruster until the fuel was gone. Pioneer 11 (1995, retired): The first spacecraft to fly past Saturn. NEAR Shoemaker (2001, retired): The first spacecraft to orbit an asteroid, and then the first to land on one. Pioneer 10 (2003, retired): The first spacecraft through the asteroid belt and past Jupiter, now a silent ghost ship. Philae (2014, lost): The first landing on a comet bounced into the shadows. Dawn (2018, retired): An ion-powered explorer that orbited two worlds and now circles Ceres in silence. Kepler Space Telescope (2018, retired): The planet hunter that showed the night sky holds more planets than stars.","link":{"label":"Museum catalog","target":"#catalog"}},{"id":"place-earth","type":"place","title":"Earth overview","place":"earth","keys":"Earth земля overview всё обзор","text":"Earth — Some stations were brought down to burn up over the ocean. The museum has 2 cards here: Skylab (1979, lost): America’s first space station fell back to Earth, scattering debris over Western Australia. Mir space station (2001, retired): A controlled ending: Mir was steered into the remote South Pacific.","link":{"label":"Museum catalog","target":"#catalog"}},{"id":"place-archive","type":"place","title":"Archives on Earth overview","place":"archive","keys":"Archives on Earth архив overview всё обзор","text":"Archives on Earth — Some things were lost on Earth, in archives. The museum has 1 cards here: The original Apollo 11 tapes (2009, lost): The original high-quality recordings of the first Moon walk were probably recorded over.","link":{"label":"Museum catalog","target":"#catalog"}},{"id":"mc-luna9","type":"moon-mission","title":"Luna 9","place":"moon","keys":"Luna 9 луна-9 луна 9  moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Luna 9 (USSR), landed 3 Feb 1966 at Oceanus Procellarum (Ocean of Storms) (7.08, -64.37). Robotic lander. Left on the Moon: The 99 kg ball-shaped landing capsule, with its four petals opened. First spacecraft to make a soft landing on the Moon and send pictures from its surface. Fun fact: Before Luna 9, some scientists worried a lander might sink into deep Moon dust. It didn't! Timeline: 1966: Landed and sent the first photos from the lunar surface until its batteries ran out on 6 Feb 1966. 1967: Batteries empty. The little capsule is still out there, but nobody has spotted it for sure yet. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunarussr.html>","link":{"label":"Luna 9 in Moon Mission Control","target":"#moon"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunarussr.html"},{"id":"mc-surveyor1","type":"moon-mission","title":"Surveyor 1","place":"moon","keys":"Surveyor 1  сервейер-1 сервейер 1 сёрвейер moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Surveyor 1 (NASA (USA)), landed 2 Jun 1966 at Inside a crater north of Flamsteed, Oceanus Procellarum (-2.4745, -43.3398). Robotic lander. Left on the Moon: The Surveyor 1 lander with its TV camera. First American soft landing on the Moon. Fun fact: It sent back more than 11,000 pictures to help NASA choose safe places for Apollo astronauts. Timeline: 1966: Landed softly and took thousands of pictures of the ground around it. 1967: Last contact on 7 Jan 1967. LRO later photographed it from orbit. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1966-045A>","link":{"label":"Surveyor 1","target":"#catalog?item=surveyor-1"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1966-045A"},{"id":"mc-surveyor3","type":"moon-mission","title":"Surveyor 3","place":"moon","keys":"Surveyor 3  сервейер-3 сервейер 3 сёрвейер moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Surveyor 3 (NASA (USA)), landed 20 Apr 1967 at Oceanus Procellarum, on a 14° slope inside a small crater (-3.0162, -23.418). Robotic lander. Left on the Moon: The Surveyor 3 lander (minus parts taken home by Apollo 12). First spacecraft to dig in lunar soil with a scoop, and the only robot ever visited by astronauts on the Moon. Fun fact: Its engines didn't switch off in time, so it bounced before landing. In 1969 the Apollo 12 crew walked over and took its camera back to Earth. Timeline: 1967: Bounced, landed, dug trenches up to 18 cm deep and sent 6,326 pictures until early May 1967. 1968: Lunar night ended the mission. It waited alone for two and a half years. 1969: Apollo 12 astronauts Pete Conrad and Alan Bean walked to it and brought its TV camera and other parts home. 1970: Still standing next to the Apollo 12 site, about 180 m away. LRO has photographed both together. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1967-035A>","link":{"label":"Surveyor 3","target":"#catalog?item=surveyor-3"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1967-035A"},{"id":"mc-apollo11","type":"moon-mission","title":"Apollo 11","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 11  аполлон-11 аполлон 11 армстронг олдрин база спокойствия море спокойствия флаг moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Apollo 11 (NASA (USA)), landed 20 Jul 1969 at Tranquility Base, Mare Tranquillitatis (Sea of Tranquility) (0.67416, 23.47314). Crewed landing · Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin (Michael Collins in orbit). Left on the Moon: Descent stage of the lunar module Eagle, with a plaque: “We came in peace for all mankind”; Laser Ranging Retroreflector (mirror for Earth's lasers); Passive seismometer (moonquake detector); US flag (Aldrin saw it blown over by the engine blast at lift-off; LRO images show no flag shadow there); A silicon disc with goodwill messages from leaders of 73 countries, and an Apollo 1 mission patch. First humans to walk on the Moon. Fun fact: Scientists on Earth still fire lasers at the Apollo 11 mirror to measure the Moon's distance – it is slowly moving away, about 3.8 cm a year. Timeline: 1969: Armstrong and Aldrin walked here for about 2.5 hours and set up experiments. 1970: The seismometer stopped in Aug 1969, but the laser mirror still works today. No batteries needed! Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-059C>","link":{"label":"Apollo 11","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-11-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-059C"},{"id":"mc-apollo12","type":"moon-mission","title":"Apollo 12","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 12  аполлон-12 аполлон 12 интрепид alsep moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Apollo 12 (NASA (USA)), landed 19 Nov 1969 at Oceanus Procellarum, ~180 m from Surveyor 3 (-3.0128, -23.4219). Crewed landing · Pete Conrad, Alan Bean (Richard Gordon in orbit). Left on the Moon: Descent stage of the lunar module Intrepid; ALSEP science station (seismometer and other instruments); US flag (LRO images from 2012 show it still casts a shadow). First precision landing – right next to a robot that had landed 2.5 years earlier. Fun fact: After the crew left, the ascent stage was crashed into the Moon on purpose. The seismometer felt the first artificial moonquake. Timeline: 1969: Conrad and Bean landed within walking distance of Surveyor 3. 1970: The ALSEP science station sent data about moonquakes and the solar wind. 1977: All ALSEP stations were switched off on 30 Sep 1977. The flag is still standing. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C>","link":{"label":"Apollo 12","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-12-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C"},{"id":"mc-lunokhod1","type":"moon-mission","title":"Lunokhod 1","place":"moon","keys":"Lunokhod 1 луноход-1 луноход 1 луноход  moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Lunokhod 1 (USSR (Luna 17)), landed 17 Nov 1970 at Mare Imbrium (Sea of Rains) (38.3158, -35.005). Robotic rover. Left on the Moon: The Luna 17 landing platform; The Lunokhod 1 rover with a French-made laser reflector. First remote-controlled rover to drive on another world. Fun fact: It drove 10.5 km over 322 days. Then it was lost for almost 40 years until NASA's LRO camera found it in 2010. Timeline: 1970: Rolled off its lander and started driving, steered by a team on Earth. 1972: Last heard from on 14 Sep 1971. Nobody knew exactly where it had parked. 2010: LRO photos showed the rover and its tracks. On 22 Apr 2010 a laser from Earth bounced off its reflector again! 2011: Its reflector is used again for laser ranging experiments from Earth. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunarussr.html>","link":{"label":"Lunokhod 1 in Moon Mission Control","target":"#moon"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/lunarussr.html"},{"id":"mc-apollo15","type":"moon-mission","title":"Apollo 15","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 15  луномобиль lrv ровер аполлон-15 машина moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Apollo 15 (NASA (USA)), landed 30 Jul 1971 at Hadley–Apennine, at the foot of the Apennine mountains (26.13239, 3.6333). Crewed landing · David Scott, James Irwin (Alfred Worden in orbit). Left on the Moon: Descent stage of the lunar module Falcon; Lunar Roving Vehicle – the first Moon car; ALSEP science station and a laser retroreflector; “Fallen Astronaut” statuette and a plaque honouring 14 astronauts and cosmonauts who died; The falcon feather that Scott dropped with a hammer to show they fall at the same speed. First mission to use a Lunar Roving Vehicle. Fun fact: David Scott dropped a hammer and a feather at the same time. With no air, they hit the ground together – just like Galileo predicted! Timeline: 1971: Scott and Irwin drove the first Moon car and explored for three days. 1972: The ALSEP station sent science data back to Earth. 1977: ALSEP switched off on 30 Sep 1977, but the laser reflector still works today. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-063C>","link":{"label":"Apollo 15","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-15-lrv"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-063C"},{"id":"mc-apollo17","type":"moon-mission","title":"Apollo 17","place":"moon","keys":"Apollo 17  аполлон-17 таурус литтров сернан moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Apollo 17 (NASA (USA)), landed 11 Dec 1972 at Taurus–Littrow valley (20.1911, 30.7723). Crewed landing · Eugene Cernan, Harrison Schmitt (Ronald Evans in orbit). Left on the Moon: Descent stage of the lunar module Challenger, with a plaque; Lunar Roving Vehicle; ALSEP science station; US flag (still standing in LRO images). Last time humans walked on the Moon. Schmitt was the first scientist (a geologist) on the Moon. Fun fact: Gene Cernan's footprints are the last human footprints on the Moon. With no wind, they could last a very long time. Timeline: 1972: Cernan and Schmitt explored the valley for three days and left on 14 Dec 1972. 1973: The ALSEP station kept sending science data. 1977: Switched off on 30 Sep 1977. Nobody has walked on the Moon since. Source: <https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1972-096C>","link":{"label":"Apollo 17","target":"#catalog?item=apollo-17-site"},"url":"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1972-096C"},{"id":"mc-chandrayaan3","type":"moon-mission","title":"Chandrayaan-3","place":"moon","keys":"Chandrayaan-3 чандраян-3 чандраян 3 викрам прагьян  moon луна","text":"Moon Mission Control pin: Chandrayaan-3 (ISRO (India)), landed 23 Aug 2023 at Near the lunar south pole, near Manzinus crater (-69.373, 32.319). Robotic lander (Vikram) and rover (Pragyan). Left on the Moon: Vikram lander; Pragyan rover; NASA's tiny Laser Retroreflector Array (5 cm wide) on Vikram. First landing near the lunar south pole. Fun fact: On 12 Dec 2023, NASA's LRO hit Vikram's tiny NASA mirror with a laser from 100 km away – the first time a spacecraft did this at the Moon. Timeline: 2023: Vikram and Pragyan worked for about two weeks, then went to sleep for the freezing lunar night. 2024: They did not wake up, but NASA's mirror on Vikram is now a permanent marker near the south pole. Source: <https://science.nasa.gov/missions/lro/laser-instrument-on-nasas-lro-successfully-pings-indian-moon-lander/>","link":{"label":"Chandrayaan-3 in Moon Mission Control","target":"#moon"},"url":"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/lro/laser-instrument-on-nasas-lro-successfully-pings-indian-moon-lander/"},{"id":"story","type":"section","title":"Scroll Story: Ghosts of exploration","keys":"story scroll история сюжет глобус ghosts chapters главы","text":"The Story section (#story) is a scroll-driven journey of ten chapters, Moon and Mars only, in chronological order. A 3D globe switches between the Moon and Mars and pins each landing site; each chapter has a button to open its story card. Chapters: 1. Scouts for the astronauts (moon, card surveyor-1): Before people could land on the Moon, robots had to prove it was possible. Surveyor 1 touched down gently in 1966 — and it is still standing there. 2. Footprints and a descent stage (moon, card apollo-11-site): Every Apollo crew flew home in the top half of their lander. The bottom half — the descent stage — stayed behind, with experiments around it. 3. Three cars parked on the Moon (moon, card apollo-15-lrv): Apollo 15, 16 and 17 each brought an electric Lunar Roving Vehicle. All three are still parked where the astronauts left them. 4. The first soft landing on Mars (mars, card mars-3): In December 1971 the Soviet Mars 3 lander became the first spacecraft to land softly on Mars. It started to transmit — and went silent within seconds. 5. The first long-lived Mars landers (mars, card viking-1): Viking 1 worked on Mars for more than six years. It stopped on 11 November 1982 after a faulty command was sent from Earth. 6. When the numbers don’t match (mars, card mco): Not everything retires peacefully. In 1999 Mars Climate Orbiter was lost at Mars because one team used imperial units and another used metric. 7. Frozen by the arctic winter (mars, card phoenix): Phoenix dug into the ice-rich soil near Mars’ north pole. When winter came, there was not enough sunlight to keep it alive. 8. Stuck in the sand (mars, card spirit): Spirit was planned to work for 90 sols and lasted over six years. In the end it got trapped in soft sand at a place called Troy. 9. Buried by dust (mars, card insight): InSight listened to more than 1,300 marsquakes until dust covered its solar panels; it fell silent in December 2022. It is Campaign 1 in our Simulator. 10. A new generation tries (moon, card vikram): In 2019 India’s Vikram lander lost contact moments before touchdown near the lunar south pole. Its debris was later found in images from NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter.","link":{"label":"Story","target":"#story"}},{"id":"why","type":"section","title":"Why missions end","keys":"why causes reasons почему причины конец миссии заканчиваются","text":"Why missions end (#why): nine cause cards, each with a Details drawer listing the real cases from the museum. Unit & specification errors: Two teams, two systems of measurement. When software hands numbers across an interface without agreeing on units, a spacecraft can be steered into a planet. Cases: mco. Software & command errors: A single wrong value, an unexpected sensor reading or a command written to the wrong memory address can end a mission that hardware alone would have survived. Cases: mpl, schiaparelli, mgs, clementine, viking-1, hakuto-r. Loss of communication: A spacecraft that cannot talk to Earth is lost even if it is intact. Antennas blocked, transmitters dead or ground commands misinterpreted all mean silence. Cases: beagle-2, mars-3, envisat. Entry, descent & landing failures: Landing is the riskiest minute of a planetary mission: thin atmospheres, unknown terrain and no chance for humans to intervene in real time. Cases: mars-2, luna-15, vikram, beresheet, luna-25, philae. Collisions & fragmentation: At orbital speeds even a small fragment carries enormous energy. Collisions and explosions turn one object into thousands. Cases: iridium-kosmos, fengyun-1c. Human error during spacewalks: In microgravity anything not tied down drifts away. Gloves, tool bags and cameras have all slipped out of astronauts’ hands. Cases: piper-bag, ed-white-glove. Fuel & power failures: Batteries freeze, solar panels get buried in dust, propellant lines rupture. Without energy, a spacecraft goes quiet forever. Cases: opportunity, spirit, mars-pathfinder, mars-observer. Abandonment at end of life: Many objects are not lost by accident. They are simply left behind when the mission ends: dead satellites, upper stages, lunar descent stages. Cases: vanguard-1, skylab, apollo-11-site, apollo-15-hammer, apollo-14-golf. Data & archive loss: Things are lost on Earth too. Tapes are recycled, formats become unreadable, and irreplaceable recordings disappear in storage. Cases: apollo-11-tapes.","link":{"label":"Why missions end","target":"#why"}},{"id":"prevent","type":"section","title":"How to prevent it","keys":"prevent prevention lessons checklist предотвратить уроки чеклист мусор","text":"How to prevent it (#prevent): lessons turned into a checklist; each card has a Checklist drawer. Verify, test, and test again (Engineering): Independently check every interface between teams and software modules (the Mars Climate Orbiter board’s central lesson). “Test as you fly”: run end-to-end simulations with realistic sensor data, including saturated or out-of-range values (Schiaparelli inquiry). Review every command and software upload before it is sent; keep a way to roll it back (Mars Global Surveyor). One system of units (Standards): Use SI (metric) units across the whole project and state units explicitly in every data file and interface document. Make units part of the data type in software, so a mismatch fails at build time instead of at Mars. Redundancy & telemetry (Design): Send telemetry during entry, descent and landing — Mars Polar Lander sent none, so nobody could say for certain what happened. Fly backup computers, sensors and radios, and design a safe mode that keeps the spacecraft pointed at the Sun and Earth. Make the antenna work even if a deployment step fails (the Beagle 2 lesson). Tethers & spacewalk procedures (Human spaceflight): Tether every tool and bag to the suit or the station — the basic rule of EVA work. Use checklists that confirm each tether before opening a bag or letting go of an item. Train in neutral-buoyancy pools with the same tools, gloves and bags used in orbit. Debris mitigation rules (Policy): 25-year guideline: spacecraft in low Earth orbit should leave orbit within 25 years after the mission ends (U.S. Government Orbital Debris Mitigation Standard Practices). 5-year rule: in 2022 the U.S. FCC adopted a rule requiring LEO satellites it licenses to deorbit within 5 years of ending their mission. Passivation: vent leftover propellant and discharge batteries so dead objects cannot explode later. Move geostationary satellites to a graveyard orbit above the GEO belt at end of life. Planned deorbiting (Operations): Keep enough fuel at the end of the mission for a final burn into the atmosphere. Steer large objects into the remote South Pacific, as was done with Mir in 2001, instead of letting them fall at random like Skylab in 1979. Design for demise: choose materials that burn up completely on re-entry. Active debris removal (New technology): ClearSpace-1 (ESA-procured mission) — designed to capture and deorbit a defunct object. ELSA-d (Astroscale, launched 2021) — demonstrated magnetic capture of a client satellite in orbit. ADRAS-J (Astroscale, 2024) — approached and inspected a discarded Japanese H-IIA upper stage at close range. RemoveDEBRIS (University of Surrey, 2018) — tested a net and a harpoon on targets in orbit. Tracking & sharing orbits (Space situational awareness): Space-Track.org publishes the U.S. Space Force catalog of tracked objects. CelesTrak redistributes orbital elements openly — this site uses them for its live globe. Commercial radar networks such as LeoLabs track objects in low Earth orbit. Collision avoidance: the ISS manoeuvres when the risk of a hit exceeds 1 in 10,000 (NASA ODPO). Archive the data forever (Data): Deposit raw and processed data in long-term public archives such as NASA’s Planetary Data System and NSSDCA. Migrate data off ageing media and formats before the readers disappear (the Apollo 11 tapes lesson). Keep several copies in different places, with documentation that explains how to read them. Open data enables discovery: citizens and scientists found Beagle 2 and Vikram in public orbiter images.","link":{"label":"Prevent","target":"#prevent"}},{"id":"numbers","type":"section","title":"The debris problem in numbers","keys":"numbers debris statistics мусор цифры статистика обломки сколько","text":"Numbers (#numbers), from the NASA Orbital Debris Program Office: ≈50,000 large, trackable objects orbiting Earth; 34,000+ objects catalogued by the U.S. Space Surveillance Network (Aug 2026); ≈100 million estimated millimetre-sized debris pieces; 7–8 km/s speed of debris in low Earth orbit; ≈10 km/s average relative impact speed; 41 ISS debris-avoidance manoeuvres since 1999. How long debris stays up: below 600 km: several years; ≈800 km: centuries; above 1,000 km: 1,000+ years. Source <https://orbitaldebris.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/>","link":{"label":"Numbers","target":"#numbers"}},{"id":"sources","type":"section","title":"Sources & credits","keys":"sources credits источники данные nasa data resources challenge","text":"Sources (#sources): searchable accordions; every fact links to its source. Data sources: NASA Orbital Debris Program Office (ODPO) — FAQ & statistics; NASA ODPO — Orbital Debris Quarterly News; CelesTrak — NORAD GP element sets (live debris & satellites); NASA NSSDCA — NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive (mission records); NASA NSSDCA — Anthropogenic impacts & spacecraft on the Moon; NASA Solar System Treks — Moon LRO WAC mosaic & Mars Viking MDIM 2.1 / MOLA tiles; NASA GIBS — Blue Marble shaded relief & bathymetry tiles; NASA 3D Resources — GLB models (public domain); NASA Image and media usage guidelines; Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter Camera (LROC) — impact site images; The Planetary Society — Mars landing sites map (2020); satellite.js — SGP4 orbit propagation. Official challenge resources used: NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive (NSSDCA) (apollo-11-site, apollo-12-site, apollo-15-lrv); Apollo Lunar Modules (descent stages) (apollo-11-site, apollo-12-site, apollo-16-site, apollo-17-site); Lunar Roving Vehicles (LRVs) (apollo-15-lrv); ALSEP scientific packages (apollo-12-site); Surveyor landers (surveyor-1, surveyor-3, surveyor-7); Sojourner rover (Mars Pathfinder) (mars-pathfinder); Spirit & Opportunity (spirit, opportunity); Viking 1 & 2 landers (viking-1, viking-2); Phoenix & InSight landers (phoenix, insight); Dawn spacecraft (dawn); NEAR Shoemaker (near-shoemaker); Pioneer 10 & 11 (pioneer-10, pioneer-11); Ingenuity helicopter (ingenuity); Kepler Space Telescope (kepler)","link":{"label":"Sources","target":"#sources"}},{"id":"lab","type":"section","title":"3D Lab","keys":"3d lab model модель лаборатория 3д крутить вращать","text":"3D Lab (#lab): pick an object from the list and drag to rotate, scroll or pinch to zoom. Moon objects stand on grey regolith, Mars objects on rusty soil, space objects float in a starfield. Models are cached after the first load. Objects: Ed White’s spare glove (NASA model); Tranquility Base leftovers (NASA model); The Apollo 15 hammer & falcon feather (NASA model); Viking 2 lander (NASA model); Skylab (NASA model); Clementine (NASA model); Mir space station (NASA model); Mars Global Surveyor (NASA model); The lost tool bag (NASA model); Spirit rover (NASA model); InSight lander (NASA model); Vanguard 1 (procedural reconstruction); Surveyor 1 (procedural reconstruction); Alan Shepard’s golf balls (procedural reconstruction); Apollo 15 Lunar Roving Vehicle (procedural reconstruction); Beagle 2 (procedural reconstruction); Envisat (procedural reconstruction); Philae (procedural reconstruction). NASA models come from NASA 3D Resources (public domain); procedural ones are coded reconstructions, not official models. Ground texture: Poly Haven \"Rocks Ground 09\" (CC0).","link":{"label":"3D Lab","target":"#lab"}},{"id":"game","type":"section","title":"Survival Simulator","keys":"game simulator play survival игра симулятор играть как играть кампания управление","text":"Survival Simulator (#game), a retro mission-control terminal. How to play: pick a campaign (click or keys 1–3); each event is a real problem from the mission; choose a decision with the mouse or number keys (1–3), Enter skips the intro. Gauges: battery, temperature, dust, integrity — if battery or integrity hits 0 the mission ends. Choices marked as the real NASA decision count toward matching the real record; the score combines how long you survived versus the real record, how many real decisions you matched and science return. At the end you see where the spacecraft is now, what is left inside it, and a shareable report link. Sound can be toggled. Gauge numbers are a game model; events, dates and decisions are real and sourced. Campaigns: InSight: Battle with Dust — Keep a solar-powered seismometer alive on Mars while dust slowly buries its panels. (NASA InSight lander, 2018–2022; 9 events; plan: Prime mission (one Mars year) 669 sols; real record: Real InSight (last contact 15 Dec 2022) 1440 sols) | Spirit: Trapped in the Sands of Troy — A rover with a broken wheel, a hidden discovery, and a winter it must face standing still. (NASA Mars Exploration Rover Spirit (MER-A), 2004–2010; 5 events; plan: Prime mission 90 sols; real record: Real Spirit (last contact 22 Mar 2010) 2208 sols) | Surveyor 3: Into the Lunar Night — A lunar scout that bounced on landing, tested the soil for Apollo — and was visited by astronauts years later. (NASA Surveyor 3 lander, 1967–1969; 3 events; plan: One lunar day of operations 14 days; real record: Real Surveyor 3 (last contact 4 May 1967) 14 days)","link":{"label":"Play the Survival Simulator","target":"#game"}},{"id":"game-insight","type":"campaign","title":"InSight: Battle with Dust","keys":"InSight: Battle with Dust NASA InSight lander инсайт сейсмометр марсотрясения game игра кампания","text":"Simulator campaign \"InSight: Battle with Dust\": Keep a solar-powered seismometer alive on Mars while dust slowly buries its panels. Events: 26 Nov 2018 Power on the surface — real decision: Deploy both solar arrays now, on landing day; 19 Dec 2018 Where should the seismometer live? — real decision: Lift SEIS with the arm and set it on the Martian ground; Feb 2019 – Jan 2021 The mole will not dig — real decision: Use the arm’s scoop to press on and pin the mole for friction; 2020 The panels are getting dusty — real decision: Pulse the array deployment motors to shake the dust off; 22 May 2021 Fight dust with dirt — real decision: Trickle sand near — not on — a panel at noon, the windiest time; 7 Jan 2022 Regional dust storm — real decision: Stay in safe mode, keep instruments off and wait for the sky to clear; 24 Apr 2022 One last picture — real decision: Take a final selfie, then park the arm in its “retirement pose”; May 2022 What gets the last watts? — real decision: Prioritise the seismometer, running at selected times such as night; 15 Dec 2022 Listening for a heartbeat — real decision: Keep listening — declare the end only after two missed passes. What really happened: 26 Nov 2018: Landed in Elysium Planitia; solar arrays opened the same day. 19 Dec 2018: Seismometer placed on the surface by the robotic arm. 28 Feb 2019 – 9 Jan 2021: Heat probe (“mole”) campaign; ended after it could not dig deeper. 22 May 2021: Sand-trickling trick gave ~30 Wh per sol of extra power. 7–19 Jan 2022: Safe mode during a regional dust storm. 24 Apr 2022: Final selfie (sol 1211). 4 May 2022: Largest marsquake recorded (sol 1222). 15 Dec 2022: Last contact. Mission declared over after two missed passes. Now: Silent on the surface since 15 December 2022, covered in dust. NASA continued to listen for a signal after retiring the mission.","link":{"label":"InSight: Battle with Dust","target":"#game"}},{"id":"game-spirit","type":"campaign","title":"Spirit: Trapped in the Sands of Troy","keys":"Spirit: Trapped in the Sands of Troy NASA Mars Exploration Rover Spirit (MER-A) спирит game игра кампания","text":"Simulator campaign \"Spirit: Trapped in the Sands of Troy\": A rover with a broken wheel, a hidden discovery, and a winter it must face standing still. Events: 2006 The right-front wheel stops — real decision: Drive mostly backwards, dragging the dead wheel behind; Spring 2007 A bright streak in the wheel track — real decision: Stop, drive back and touch it with the arm’s X-ray spectrometer; 1 May 2009 Wheels sink at Troy — real decision: Stop driving. Study the soil and rehearse escapes with a test rover on Earth; 26 Jan 2010 Winter is coming to Gusev — real decision: Become a stationary platform; use the last drives to tilt the panels north; 22 Mar 2010 Hibernation — real decision: Keep listening through the winter and into spring. What really happened: 4 Jan 2004: Landed in Gusev Crater. 2006: Right-front wheel stopped; Spirit drove backwards dragging it. 2007: Dragging wheel exposed ~90% silica soil — evidence of past water. Apr–May 2009: Wheels broke through a crust at “Troy” and dug into soft sand. 26 Jan 2010: Designated a stationary research platform. 22 Mar 2010: Last communication (sol 2208). 25 May 2011: NASA ended recovery efforts. Now: Still sitting at Troy, embedded in the sand where it last worked, silent since March 2010.","link":{"label":"Spirit: Trapped in the Sands of Troy","target":"#game"}},{"id":"game-surveyor3","type":"campaign","title":"Surveyor 3: Into the Lunar Night","keys":"Surveyor 3: Into the Lunar Night NASA Surveyor 3 lander сервейер-3 сервейер 3 сёрвейер game игра кампания","text":"Simulator campaign \"Surveyor 3: Into the Lunar Night\": A lunar scout that bounced on landing, tested the soil for Apollo — and was visited by astronauts years later. Events: 20 Apr 1967 The engine will not stop — real decision: Let it settle — then check health before doing anything; Late April 1967 Will the ground hold Apollo? — real decision: Dig trenches and run bearing and impact tests in front of the camera; 2–4 May 1967 Sunset on the Ocean of Storms — real decision: Keep the link open until the last possible moment. What really happened: 17 Apr 1967: Launch. 20 Apr 1967: Bounced twice, then landed in Oceanus Procellarum. Late Apr 1967: Four trenches dug; soil judged strong enough for Apollo. 4 May 1967: Last contact, two days into the lunar night. 19 Nov 1969: Apollo 12 astronauts visited and removed the camera and scoop. Now: Still standing in Surveyor crater, minus its camera and scoop. NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has imaged it next to the Apollo 12 descent stage and the astronauts’ footpaths.","link":{"label":"Surveyor 3: Into the Lunar Night","target":"#game"}},{"id":"page-hero","type":"page","title":"Page section: hero","keys":"hero section раздел avantgarde авангард команда team space apps","text":"Section #hero text: Team Avantgarde · NASA Space Apps 2026 Abandoned but not Forgotten. They stopped working. They are still out there. Dozens of NASA landers, rovers and science stations sit silent on the Moon and Mars. Take the console of mission control and try to keep InSight, Spirit and Surveyor 3 alive through the same problems the real engineers faced — then visit every “ghost” site on real NASA maps and learn what each machine was for and what it discovered. ▶ Open Moon Mission Control Play the Survival Simulator","link":null},{"id":"page-moon","type":"page","title":"Page section: moon","keys":"moon section раздел moon mission control slider year timeline слайдер год луна карта пины рельеф terrain","text":"Section #moon text: Mission Control · The Moon, 1966 → today Travel through time on the Moon Spin the real Moon, built from NASA Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter data. Every pin is a machine people left behind. Move the slider from 1966 to today and watch each one land, work, go silent, get lost and even get found again. Guide · Pip Pip lands here and reacts to every pin you tap and every year you visit. ▶ Take the tour with Pip Live · Lunar nearside Surface Terrain + − ⟲ Loading the Moon… Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Mission file Travel through time 1966 1966 1977 1990 2010 Today Mission files Abandoned but not Forgotten On the Moon there is no wind and no rain, so what we leave there can last for a very long time. Some of it still works: the laser mirrors of Apollo 11, Apollo 15, Lunokhod 1 and Chandrayaan-3 still help scientists measure the Moon today.","link":{"label":"moon","target":"#moon"}},{"id":"page-game","type":"page","title":"Page section: game","keys":"game section раздел","text":"Section #game text: Main experience Survival Simulator You are the operator. Each event is a real problem from the mission. Make the decisions NASA’s engineers made to reach the real survival record — or take another path and unlock the science of why it fails. When the mission ends, find out where the spacecraft is now. MISSION CONTROL TERMINAL · v1.0 🔊 SOUND ⟲ CAMPAIGNS Gauges are a simplified game model , not real NASA telemetry or commands. Events, dates and outcomes come from the NASA sources linked in each step.","link":{"label":"game","target":"#game"}},{"id":"page-story","type":"page","title":"Page section: story","keys":"story section раздел","text":"Section #story text: Scroll story Ghosts of exploration For more than half a century we have been leaving machines on the Moon and Mars — on purpose and by accident. Scroll through ten of their stories.","link":{"label":"story","target":"#story"}},{"id":"page-catalog","type":"page","title":"Page section: catalog","keys":"catalog section раздел catalog museum каталог музей карточки фильтр","text":"Section #catalog text: Museum catalog Museum of left-behind things Retired NASA hardware on the Moon, on Mars and in deep space, plus the missions that were lost on the way — each with a story card, a 3D model and sources.","link":{"label":"catalog","target":"#catalog"}},{"id":"page-atlas","type":"page","title":"Page section: atlas","keys":"atlas section раздел atlas map карта атлас пины ghost sites","text":"Section #atlas text: Interactive atlas · NASA tiles Ghost-site map Zoom into real NASA mosaics — the Moon from the LRO Wide Angle Camera and Mars from Viking and MOLA via NASA Solar System Treks (Earth from NASA GIBS). Lavender pins are retired hardware; pink pins are lost or crashed missions. Click a pin, or use the list, to open its story. Moon Mars Earth (re-entries)","link":{"label":"atlas","target":"#atlas"}},{"id":"page-lab","type":"page","title":"Page section: lab","keys":"lab section раздел","text":"Section #lab text: Three.js · NASA 3D Resources 3D Lab: hold the hardware Drag to rotate, scroll or pinch to zoom. Models marked NASA are official public-domain models; others are procedural reconstructions.","link":{"label":"lab","target":"#lab"}},{"id":"page-orbit","type":"page","title":"Page section: orbit","keys":"orbit section раздел live orbit debris satellite sgp4 celestrak орбита мусор спутники обломки","text":"Section #orbit text: CelesTrak · satellite.js Context: what happens when hardware is left in Earth orbit On the Moon and Mars, retired machines stand still. Around Earth they keep moving — and breaking up. Every dot is a real tracked fragment, positioned with the SGP4 model from CelesTrak orbital elements. Hover a dot to identify it.","link":{"label":"orbit","target":"#orbit"}},{"id":"page-why","type":"page","title":"Page section: why","keys":"why section раздел","text":"Section #why text: Causes Why missions end Nine recurring reasons — from planned retirement to a single wrong number — each with real cases from the museum.","link":{"label":"why","target":"#why"}},{"id":"page-numbers","type":"page","title":"Page section: numbers","keys":"numbers section раздел","text":"Section #numbers text: NASA Orbital Debris Program Office The debris problem in numbers How long does debris stay up? Source: NASA ODPO FAQ","link":{"label":"numbers","target":"#numbers"}},{"id":"page-prevent","type":"page","title":"Page section: prevent","keys":"prevent section раздел","text":"Section #prevent text: Solutions How to prevent it Lessons written in left-behind hardware — turned into a checklist.","link":{"label":"prevent","target":"#prevent"}},{"id":"page-disclosure","type":"page","title":"Page section: disclosure","keys":"disclosure section раздел ai disclosure ии нейросеть как сделан","text":"Section #disclosure text: Transparency How this was made · Super Intelligence (SI / AI) use disclosure AI-assisted Source code (Vite, Three.js, Leaflet, satellite.js) was written with an AI coding assistant and reviewed and tested by the team. Draft text (story cards, event descriptions, prompts) was written with AI assistance, then checked line by line against the linked NASA and peer-reviewed sources. Procedural 3D reconstructions (marked “procedural”) were coded, not generated by an image model. Pip, the guide, is a hand-drawn SVG/CSS character; his scripted lines were written with AI help and checked by the team. Ask Pip chat: when you type a question to Pip, the answer is generated live by a large language model (an open Gemma / Nemotron model or Google Gemini, reached through OpenRouter) via our own server proxy (a Supabase Edge Function). Answers are AI-generated, not checked by the team, and can be wrong — always check the linked NASA sources. We send only your question and the last few chat lines; nothing is stored. If the AI is unavailable, Pip answers from a small built-in fact list instead. Not AI-generated No AI-generated images, video, voices or music. Sound effects are synthesised beeps in the browser. All photographs, map tiles and “NASA model” 3D files are NASA (and partner) public-domain media, credited where shown. No NASA logos, insignia or mission patches are used in any media we made. Limits Gauge values and scores are a game model. Map markers mark landing or descent-stage positions from published tables; rovers may have moved from these points. Not affiliated with or endorsed by NASA. Fact or game? Real: every event, date, decision, outcome, coordinate and photo, with a linked NASA or peer-reviewed source. Game model: the gauge numbers (battery, temperature, dust, integrity) and the score. Accessibility Fully keyboard-playable (number keys pick choices, Tab moves through everything). Each 3D view has a text description; every map pin is also a list button; screen readers hear each event and outcome. Motion is reduced when the system “reduce motion” setting is on. Sound is optional. Pip can be dragged anywhere (double-tap to send him home) and his chat works with the keyboard.","link":{"label":"disclosure","target":"#disclosure"}},{"id":"page-sources","type":"page","title":"Page section: sources","keys":"sources section раздел","text":"Section #sources text: Open data Sources & credits Every fact links to its source. Images: NASA/JPL-Caltech and partners as credited; NASA media is generally not copyrighted (see NASA guidelines). Filter sources Open all Official challenge resources (NASA) 0 Every item on the challenge’s NASA Data & Resources list, and where it appears here. Moon Mission Control: data 0 Moon colour map: LRO Wide Angle Camera mosaic via NASA Solar System Treks. Terrain: LRO LOLA elevation from the NASA SVS “CGI Moon Kit” (E. Wright, 2019), svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4720 . Landing dates and LRO-derived coordinates: NASA NSSDCA Master Catalog: Surveyor 1 , Surveyor 3 , Apollo 11 , Apollo 12 , Apollo 15 , Apollo 17 . Luna 9 and Luna 17 / Lunokhod 1: NASA NSSDCA, Soviet Missions to the Moon . Moon Mission Control: stories & fact checks 0 Lunokhod 1 found by LRO: Robinson et al., EPSC 2010-724 . Chandrayaan-3 mirror pinged by LRO: NASA Science ; ISRO . Objects left on the Moon: NASA History Office, Catalogue of Manmade Material on the Moon . Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter mission page. Apollo flags (Apollo 11 flag blown over; 12, 16, 17 still standing): NASA ALSJ, Six Flags on the Moon: What is Their Current Condition? ; LROC, Question Answered! (2012). Apollo 11 goodwill disc (73 countries) and Apollo 1 patch: NASA ALSJ, Apollo 11 Memorial Items ; NASA release 69-83F, goodwill messages . Moon moving away ~3.8 cm a year: NASA, The Apollo Experiment That Keeps on Giving . Fallen Astronaut memorial: NASA Science . Hammer and feather drop: NASA NSSDCA . Lunar Roving Vehicle (design speed ~13 km/h): NASA NSSDCA . Luna 9 candidate landing site (2026): Pinault, Crawford & Yano, npj Space Exploration (21 Jan 2026), a possible site still awaiting confirmation. Viking 1 end of mission (11 Nov 1982) and Opportunity’s distance record passed by Perseverance (11 Oct 2026): NASA Science, Viking 1 ; Spirit and Opportunity . Data & tiles 0 All cited pages 0 Software & credits 0 Three.js (MIT) with OrbitControls, Vite, Leaflet, satellite.js, GSAP with ScrollTrigger (GSAP standard licence) and Lenis (MIT); fonts Syne, Epilogue and IBM Plex Mono (SIL OFL) via Fontsource. 3D Lab ground: regolith texture “Rocks Ground 09” by Poly Haven (CC0), desaturated and tinted grey for the Moon and rust for Mars. Procedural models (Lunar Roving Vehicle, Surveyor and others) are our simplified reconstructions from NASA dimensions and photos, not official models; NASA GLBs are re-compressed with glTF-Transform (Draco + WebP). Pip: hand-made SVG","link":{"label":"sources","target":"#sources"}}];
